5, -0.01800537109375, -0.0167388916015625, 0.0012350082397460938, -0.00897216796875, -0.00870513916015625, -0.0138092041015625, 0.028289794921875, -0.00815582275390625, 0.0177001953125, -0.01198577880859375, 0.001773834228515625, 0.0277862548828125, 0.03350830078125, 0.0092926025390625, -0.0210418701171875, 0.0113525390625, 0.0019292831420898438, 0.0218505859375, -0.01152801513671875, 0.0059356689453125, -0.0041046142578125, -0.0289154052734375, 0.00604248046875, -0.0175323486328125, -0.004241943359375, 0.00095367431640625, 0.041168212890625, 0.028289794921875, -0.006473541259765625, -0.0201568603515625, 0.006374359130859375, 0.008544921875, -0.0022182464599609375, -0.00266265869140625, 0.01050567626953125, -0.02447509765625, 0.010467529296875, -0.0124664306640625, -0.021087646484375, 0.004619598388671875, -0.026947021484375, 0.0167999267578125, -0.054046630859375, 0.005413055419921875, -0.01245880126953125, -0.017242431640625, 0.0157928466796875, -0.029693603515625, -0.007068634033203125, -0.01016998291015625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that incrementally learn from a stream of tasks and update a textual memory bank (a persistent store of natural language summaries and feedback appended after each episode) to improve over time\u2014have demonstrated strong task performance. However, their *reliability aspects*, specifically the consistency and robustness of their performance across different sources of stochasticity (such as random seeds, sampling, or agent initialization) and variable task sequences, remain underexplored. Here, *consistency* refers to low between-run variance in agent performance, while *robustness* involves stability across changes in task ordering and stochastic elements. Furthermore, concepts like *implicit curriculum*\u2014the unintentional ordering of tasks such that learning naturally progresses from easier to harder instances (as described by Bengio et al., 2009)\u2014and *memory drift*\u2014the gradual accumulation of errors or irrelevant information in the agent's memory\u2014are key to understanding observed agent fragility, but often remain undefined in the literature.\n\nThis work systematically addresses the following research questions:\n(1) How consistent is the performance of memory-based self-improving agents under different runs and task orderings?\n(2) What are the main sources of fragility or instability in these agents' improvement processes?\n(3) Can explicit specification and feedback mitigate these weaknesses?\n\n**Experimental Setup**\nWe re-evaluate two representative memory-based methods: (a) episodic memory augmentation for language agents (e.g., agents that append text summaries of actions, errors, and corrections to a persistent bank between episodes, following [Wang et al., 2023]); and (b) reinforcement learning (RL) agents that retrieve and query a textual archive of past interactions\u2014comprising state, action, reward, and feedback trace logs\u2014to inform policy updates (cf. [Lee et al., 2022]). Each agent type uses a simple transformer-based backbone (6-layer, 512-width) and accesses a textual memory buffer limited to the most recent 100 entries. Training proceeds for 200 episodes per configuration. For environments, we use a grid-based simulated world (for procedural tasks, such as virtual object assembly) and an automatically generated reasoning benchmark featuring 4-step arithmetic or symbolic manipulation word problems. All reward structures and environment code are publicly available (see Supplementary Materials).\n\nFor evaluation, we report both (i) task completion rate (final goal state achieved per environment-defined criteria), and (ii) average episode reward (cumulative numeric reward over the episode). To ensure reproducibility and generality, we conduct ten independent training/evaluation runs per agent/method, randomizing task sequences using uniform sampling without replacement for each run, thus eliminating any fixed or ascending-difficulty order unless otherwise noted.\n\n**Results**\n\n*Performance Variance and Task Order Sensitivity*: Agent performance exhibits substantial between-run variance. Specifically, under identical hyperparameters and environment conditions, completion rates across random seeds and task orders range from 55% to 88% (mean 68%, standard deviation up to 13.7%) on procedural tasks, and from 41% to 73% (mean 56%, std. dev. 11.2%) on reasoning tasks. Statistical testing (Welch's t-test, p<0.01) confirms that these completion rate variances are significant across seeds. The *self-improving loop*\u2014wherein the agent's updated memory directly influences future actions\u2014amplifies run-to-run unpredictability, with memory errors sometimes cascading and causing unrecoverable failures (see Table 1).\n\nMoreover, improvement trajectories are highly sensitive to task order: with randomly shuffled task orders, mean completion rate gains drop by 10\u201318% (95% CI: 8.1\u201319.0%) compared to fixed or ascending-difficulty schedules. In 30% of shuffled runs, improvement is entirely absent (completion rate plateau at or below baseline; Table 2). This underscores the significant, quantifiable impact of the *implicit curriculum* present in prior studies, where easier-to-harder ordering incidentally facilitates incremental mastery and generalization, confounding true robustness (see also [Bengio et al., 2009]; [Hosseini et al., 2021] on curriculum effects in RL).\n\n**Diagnoses**\n\nManual inspection of memory traces reveals frequent misinterpretations when task instructions and feedback are ambiguous or underspecified, leading to *memory drift*. Unstructured feedback causes the agent to store vague or irrelevant entries that hinder future decision-making.\n\n**Interventions and Their Quantitative Effects**\n\nTo test mitigation strategies, we intervene in memory construction by (i) appending structured rubrics\u2014explicit textual checklists listing task success/failure criteria\u2014to agent memories, and (ii) supplying programmatic, fine-grained feedback (marking specific subtasks as passed/failed) instead of only a final binary outcome. With these interventions, worst-case completion rates increase from 55% to 63% on procedural tasks and from 41% to 49% on reasoning tasks, with improvements of 5\u20138% (p<0.05, paired t-test; Table 3). Standard deviation in completion rate decreases by 2\u20134% in most domains, but substantial between-run variance (range still 14\u201319%) and high sensitivity to task order persist. Thus, while structured specification mitigates some failures, underlying reliability limitations remain (see Figure 1 for run-level trajectories).\n\n**Connecting Findings to Research Questions**\n\n(1) *Consistency under different runs and orderings:* Agent performance shows quantifiably high variance across seeds and task orderings, obscured by reporting only mean metrics.\n(2) *Sources of fragility:* Main fragility sources are error accumulation via self-improving loops (memory drift) and high sensitivity to task order, particularly in the absence of explicit task structure.\n(3) *Effect of specification/feedback:* Explicit rubrics and fine-grained feedback yield measurable, but modest, improvements in worst-case performance and reduce total failures, though do not fully resolve instability.\n\n**Comparison with Prior Work**\n\nPrior studies on self-improving agents ([Wang et al., 2023]; [Lee et al., 2022]) focus primarily on mean performance under fixed curricula, often underreporting variance and robustness. Recent reviews ([Gao et al., 2023]; [Hosseini et al., 2021]) highlight that such fixed ordering can overstate agents' generality. Our findings align with observations in curriculum learning literature that sequence matters greatly and reliability must be stress-tested through randomization and variance reporting.\n\n**Recommendations**\n\n- (1) Future evaluations of self-improving agents should report both mean and variance of all metrics (e.g., completion rate, episode reward) across multiple random seeds and strictly randomized task orderings.\n- (2) Benchmark protocols should minimize implicit curricula by randomizing task sequences and including explicit stress-test splits with deliberately difficult or adversarial orderings.\n- (3) Agent and environment interfaces should explicitly support structured specification (e.g., through rubrics and granular programmatic feedback) to enhance both agent comprehension and external diagnostics, with quantitative reporting of failure/success distributions.\n\nWe hope these actionable recommendations\u2014directly grounded in our research questions and results\u2014will guide the development of more robust, transparent, and trustworthy memory-based self-improving agents."}}